paragraph.math:
$$A^{-1}~=~\frac{1}{\text{det \,\,}A}\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrrr}
      C_{11} & C_{21} & \ldots & C_{n1} \\
      C_{12} & C_{22} & \ldots & C_{n2} \\
       \vdots & \vdots & & \vdots \\
      C_{1n} & C_{2n} & \ldots & C_{nn}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$$

example.paragraph:
Find the inverse of the matrix $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      2 & 1 & 3 \\
      1 & {- 1} & 1 \\
      1 & 4 & {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$.

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{matrix}
      C_{11} & \left. = + \middle| \begin{array}{rr}
      {- 1} & 1 \\
      4 & {- 2}
      \end{array} \middle| = - 2\text{, }\quad \right. & C_{12} & \left. = - \middle| \begin{array}{rr}
      1 & 1 \\
      1 & {- 2}
      \end{array} \middle| = 3\text{, }\quad \right. & C_{13} & \left. = + \middle| \begin{array}{rr}
      1 & {- 1} \\
      1 & 4
      \end{array} \middle| = 5 \right. \\
      C_{21} & \left. = - \middle| \begin{array}{rr}
      1 & 3 \\
      4 & {- 2}
      \end{array} \middle| = 14\text{, }\quad \right. & C_{22} & \left. = + \middle| \begin{array}{rr}
      2 & 3 \\
      1 & {- 2}
      \end{array} \middle| = - 7\text{, }\quad \right. & C_{23} & \left. = - \middle| \begin{array}{rr}
      2 & 1 \\
      1 & 4
      \end{array} \middle| = - 7 \right. \\
      C_{31} & \left. = + \middle| \begin{array}{rr}
      1 & 3 \\
      {- 1} & 1
      \end{array} \middle| = 4\text{, }\quad \right. & C_{32} & \left. = - \middle| \begin{array}{rr}
      2 & 3 \\
      1 & 1
      \end{array} \middle| = 1\text{, }\quad \right. & C_{33} & \left. = + \middle| \begin{array}{rr}
      2 & 1 \\
      1 & {- 1}
      \end{array} \middle| = - 3 \right.
      \end{matrix}$$

note.paragraph:
Cramer’s rule is also a theoretical tool. It can be used to study how sensitive the solution of $A\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}$ is to changes in an entry in $\mathbf{b}$ or in $A$ (perhaps due to experimental error when acquiring the entries for $\mathbf{b}$ or $A$). When $A$ is a $3 \times 3$ matrix with complex entries, Cramer’s rule is sometimes selected for hand computation because row reduction of $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      A & \mathbf{b}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ with complex arithmetic can be messy, and the determinants are fairly easy to compute. For a larger $n \times n$ matrix (real or complex), Cramer’s rule is hopelessly inefficient. Computing just one determinant takes about as much work as solving $A\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}$ by row reduction.

content.paragraph:
See Figure 1. It will suffice to show that any $2 \times 2$ matrix $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      \textbf{a}_{1} & \textbf{a}_{2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ can be transformed into a diagonal matrix in a way that changes neither the area of the associated parallelogram nor $\left| \text{det }A \middle| . \right.$ From Section 3.2, we know that the absolute value of the determinant is unchanged when two columns are interchanged or a multiple of one column is added to another. And it is easy to see that such operations suffice to transform $A$ into a diagonal matrix. Column interchanges do not change the parallelogram at all. So it suffices to prove the following simple geometric observation that applies to vectors in $\text{ℝ}^{2}$ or $\text{ℝ}^{3}$:

A parallelepiped is shown in Figure 4 as a shaded box with two sloping sides. Its volume is the area of the base in the plane $\text{Span \{}\textbf{a}_{1}\text{, }\textbf{a}_{3}\text{\}}$ times the altitude of $\textbf{a}_{2}$ above $\text{Span }\{\textbf{a}_{1}\text{, }\textbf{a}_{3}\}$. Any vector $\textbf{a}_{2} + c\textbf{a}_{1}$ has the same altitude because $\textbf{a}_{2} + c\textbf{a}_{1}$ lies in the plane $\textbf{a}_{2} + \text{Span }\{\textbf{a}_{1}\text{, }\textbf{a}_{3}\}$, which is parallel to $\text{Span }\{\textbf{a}_{1}\text{, }\textbf{a}_{3}\}$. Hence the volume of the parallelepiped is unchanged when $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      \textbf{a}_{1} & \textbf{a}_{2} & \textbf{a}_{3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ is changed to $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      \textbf{a}_{1} & {\textbf{a}_{2} + c\textbf{a}_{1}} & \textbf{a}_{3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Thus a column replacement operation does not affect the volume of the parallelepiped. Since column interchanges have no effect on the volume, the proof is complete.

Consider the $2 \times 2$ case, with $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      \textbf{a}_{1} & \textbf{a}_{2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. A parallelogram at the origin in $\text{ℝ}^{2}$ determined by vectors $\textbf{b}_{1}$ and $\textbf{b}_{2}$ has the form

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{matrix}
      {T\left( s_{1}\mathbf{b}_{1} + s_{2}\mathbf{b}_{2} \right)} & {= s_{1}T\left( \mathbf{b}_{1} \right) + s_{2}T\left( \mathbf{b}_{2} \right)} \\
       & {= s_{1}A\mathbf{b}_{1} + s_{2}A\mathbf{b}_{2}}
      \end{matrix}$$

content.paragraph:
where $0 \leq s_{1} \leq 1$, $0 \leq s_{2} \leq 1$. It follows that $T(S)$ is the parallelogram determined by the columns of the matrix $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      {A\mathbf{b}_{1}} & {A\mathbf{b}_{2}}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. This matrix can be written as $A B$, where $B = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      \mathbf{b}_{1} & \mathbf{b}_{2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. By Theorem 9 and the product theorem for determinants,

